{
  "dom_id": "step-q117356",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945897/assets/10b6f63df56c789d0d28cd14caad0a2588e247ba4a60239f4cb0dd5a32777daf.png)\n\nFind $AM$ given that the area of $\u25b3ABC$ is $64\\,{m}^{2},$ $MC=11\\,m,$ and $BM=8\\,m.$}\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
  "worked_solution": "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is $b=AC,$ and the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=BM=8\\,m.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the base as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 64 & =\\frac{b\\cdot 8}{2} \\\\ 128 & =b\\cdot 8 \\\\ b & =\\frac{128}{8} \\\\ b & =16\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $AC=16\\,m$ and hence, we have\n\n$$\nAM=AC-MC=16-11=5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$",
